¶
{{smaller block|He endeavours to turn and ''wind'' himself every way to evade the force of this famous challenge. {{right|''Waterland.''}}}}

'''2.''' To bend or turn at will; direct according to one’s pleasure; vary the course or direction of; hence, to exercise complete control over.
{{smaller block|<poem>She is the clernesse and the verray light
That in this derke world me ''wynt'' and ledeth.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 85.}}
<poem>To turn and ''wind'' a fiery Pegasus,
And witch the world with noble horsemanship.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., iv. 1. 109.}}}}

'''3.''' To turn or twist round and round on something; place or arrange in more or less regular coils or convolutions on something (such as a reel, spool, or bobbin) which is turned round and round; form into a ball, hank, or the like by turning that on which successive coils are placed, or by carrying the coils round it: as, to ''wind'' yarn or thread.
{{smaller block|You have ''wound'' a goodly clew.
{{right|''Shak.'', All’s Well, i. 3. 188.}}}}

'''4†.''' To form by twisting or twining; weave; fabricate.
{{smaller block|<poem>For that same net so cunningly was ''wound''
That neither guile nor force might it distraine.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. xii. 82.}}}}

'''5.''' To place in folds, or otherwise dispose on or around something; bind; twist; wrap.
{{smaller block|This hand, just ''wound'' about thy coal-black hair.
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., v. 1. 54.}}
<poem>''Wind'' the penance-sheet
About her! {{right|''Browning'', Count Gismond.}}</poem>}}

'''6.''' To entwist; infold; encircle: literally or figuratively.

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Eche gan other in his winges take,
And with her nekkes eche gan other ''wynde''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parliament of Fowls, l. 671.}}}}
{{smaller block|Sleep thou, and I will ''wind'' thee in my arms.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iv. 1. 45.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>You talk as if you meant to ''wind'' me in,
And make me of the number.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Laws of Candy, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|M<sup>r</sup>. Allerton being ''wound'' into his debte also upon particuler dealings. ''Bradford'', Plymouth Plantation, p. 302.}}
{{smaller block|And ''wind'' the front of youth with flowers.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Ancient Sage.}}}}
'''7.''' To haul or hoist by or as by a winch, whim, capstan, or the like: as, to ''wind'' or warp a ship out of harbor; specifically, in ''mining'', to raise (the produce of the mine) to the surface by means of a winding-engine; hoist. {{smaller|The term ''wind'', as well as ''draw'', is often employed in Great Britain, while ''hoist'' is generally used in the United States. In the early days of mining, ore and coal were almost exclusively raised by hand-, horse-, or steam-power, in buckets or kibbles; at the present time, in both England and the United States, this is done by means of a winding-engine which turns a drum on which a rope (generally of steel wire) is wound and unwound, and by means of which a cage (see ''cage'', 3 (''d'')) is raised or lowered, on which the loaded cars are lifted to the surface, and the empties returned to the pit-bottom. The dimensions of engines, drums, and cages in large mines are sometimes very great, as is also the velocity with which the machinery is moved. Thus, in the Monkwearmouth colliery, Durham, England, the winding-drums are 25 feet in diameter, the rope weighs 4½ tons, the cage and load 7½ tons; the vertical distance through which the cage is raised is 580 yards, and the time occupied in lifting it and discharging the cars is two minutes and four seconds.}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|The Hollanders . . . layd out haulsers, and ''wound'' themselues out of the way of vs. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', iii. 710.}}}}
'''8.''' To insinuate; work or introduce insidiously or stealthily; worm.
{{smaller block|As he by his bould confidence & large promises deceived them in England that sente him, so he had ''wound'' him selfe in to these mens high esteeme hear. {{right|''Bradford'', Plymouth Plantation, p. 171.}}}}
{{smaller block|They have little arts and dexterities to ''wind'' in such things into discourse. {{right|''Dr. H. More.''}}}}
'''9†.''' To contrive by resort to shifts and expedients (to effect something); bring; procure or get by devious ways.
{{smaller block|<poem>Wee’ll haue some trick and wile
To ''winde'' our yonger brother out of prison
That lies in for the Rape.</poem>
{{right|''Tourneur'', Revenger’s Tragedy, iii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|He with his former dealings had ''wound'' in what money he had in y<sup>e</sup> partnership into his owne hands. {{right|''Bradford'', Plymouth Plantation, p. 301.}}}}
'''10†.''' To circulate; put or keep in circulation.
{{smaller block|Amongst the rest of the Plantations all this Summer little was done but securing themselues and planting Tobacco, which passes there as current Siluer, and by the oft turning and ''winding'' it some grow rich, but many poore. {{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith’s'' Works, II. 89.}}}}
{{smaller block|There is no State that ''winds'' the Penny more nimbly, and makes quicker Returns [than Lucca]. {{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. i. 41.}}}}
'''11.''' To adjust or dispose for work or motion by coiling a spring more tightly or otherwise turning some mechanical device: as, to ''wind'' a clock or a watch. See ''to wind up'' (''f''), below.
{{smaller block|When he ''wound'' his clock on Sunday nights the whirr of that monitor reminded the widow to ''wind'' hers. {{right|''T. Hardy'', Trumpet-Major, iii.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
To wind a ship, to bring it round until the head occupies the place where the stern was.—'''To wind off''', to unwind; uncoil.—'''To wind up'''. (''a'') To coil up into a small compass, as a skein of thread; form into a ball or coil round a bobbin, reel, or the like. Hence—(''b'') To bring to a final disposition or conclusion; finish; arrange and adjust for final settlement, as the affairs of a company or partnership on its dissolution.
{{smaller block|I could not ''wind'' it [the discourse] ''up'' closer.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. vi. 3.}}
The Author, upon the ''winding up'' of his Action, introduces all those who had any Concern in it.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 357.}}
Signor Jupe was to “enliven the varied performances at frequent intervals with his chaste Shakspearian quips and retorts.” Lastly he was to ''wind'' them ''up'' by appearing in his favourite character of Mr. William Button.
{{right|''Dickens'', Hard Times, i. 3.}}}}
(''c'') To tighten, as the strings of certain musical instruments, so as to bring them to the proper pitch; put in tune by stretching the strings over the pegs.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Wind up'' the slacken’d strings of thy lute.</poem>
{{right|''Waller'', Chloris and Hylas.}}}}
Hence, figuratively—(''d'') To restore to harmony or concord; bring to a natural or healthy condition.
{{smaller block|<poem>The untuned and jarring senses, O, ''wind up'',
Of this child-changed father! ''Shak.'', Lear, iv. 7. 16.</poem>}}
(''e'') To bring to a state of great tension; subject to a severe strain or excitement; put upon the stretch.
{{smaller block|They ''wound up'' his temper to a pitch, and treacherously made use of that infirmity.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury''.}}
Our poet was at last ''wound up'' to the height of expectation.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Voltaire.}}}}
(''f'') To bring into a state of renewed or continued motion, as a watch or clock, by coiling anew the spring or drawing up the weights.
{{smaller block|<poem>When an authentic watch is shown,</poem>}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Each man ''winds up'' and rectifies his own.</poem>
{{right|''Suckling'', Aglaura, Epil.}}}}

Hence, figuratively—(''g'') To prepare for continued movement, action, or activity; arrange or adapt for continued operation; give fresh or continued activity or energy to; restore to original vigor or order.

{{smaller block|<poem>Fate seemed to ''wind'' him ''up'' for fourscore years
Yet freshly ran he on ten winters more. ''Dryden.''</poem>
<poem>Is there a tongue like Delia’s o’er her cup,
That runs for ages without ''winding-up?''</poem>
{{right|''Young'', Love of Fame, i. 282.}}}}

(''h'') To hoist; draw; raise by or as by a winch.

{{smaller block|<poem>Let me see thy hand: this was ne’er made to wash,
Or ''wind up'' water, beat clothes, or rub floor.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Coxcomb, ii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Winding-up Act''', in ''Eng. law'', an act providing for the dissolution of joint-stock companies, and the winding up of their affairs; more specifically, 7 and 8 Vict., c. 111 (1844); followed and amended by 9 and 10 Vict., c. 28 (1846); 11 and 12 Vict., c. 45 (1848); 12 and 13 Vict., c. 108 (1849); 13 and 14 Vict., c. 83 (1850); 19 and 20 Vict., c. 47 (1856); 20 and 21 Vict., c. 49, c. 78 (1857); and superseded by The Companies’ Act (1862), 25 and 26 Vict., c. 89.}}

'''wind'''<sup>1</sup> (wīnd), ''n.'' [< ME. ''winde'' (= MD. MHG. ''winde'', OHG. ''wintā''); from the verb.] A winding; a turn; a bend: as, the road there takes a ''wind'' to the south.—{{smaller|'''Out of wind''', free from bends or crooks; perfectly straight. [Colloq.]}}

'''wind'''<sup>2</sup> (wind; formerly and still poetically also wīnd), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wind'', ''wynd'', < AS. ''wind'' = OS. OFries. D. LG. ''wind'' = OHG. MHG. ''wint'', G. ''wind'' = Icel. ''vindr'' = Sw. Dan. ''vind'' = Goth. ''winds'', ''winths'', wind, air in motion, = W. ''gwynt''

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller|stituting progressive areas of high and low pressure, the ultimate origin of which, especially of those in high latitudes, is not satisfactorily determined; (4) ''whirlwinds'' and (certain) ''squalls'', which arise when the air is in a condition of unstable equilibrium, and are developed as a part of the process by which stability is regained (this class includes the most violent winds, such as the tornado), and these occur when the instability is the combined effect of a high temperature and a high humidity, a condition favorable to the development of the greatest possible gradients of density, and hence of the most terrific manifestations of wind; (5) ''special'', winds which logically belong to the preceding classes, but which by reason of special characteristics, arising frequently from local topography, have received special appellations, as the ''sirocco'', the ''harmattan'', the ''mistral'', the ''foehn'', the ''chinook'', etc. Winds are also commonly named from the point of compass from which they blow, as a ''north wind'', an ''east wind'', a ''southwest wind''. The winds were personified and worshiped as divinities by the ancients, and representations of them are frequent in ancient art, particularly in Greek sculpture and vase-painting.}}

{{smaller block|And erly on the Tewysday, whiche was seynt Thomas daye, we made sayle, and passed by the costes of Slauony and Hystria with easy ''wynde''.
{{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 9.}}}}

{{smaller block|By reason of contrary ''windes'' we put backe againe to Prodeno, because we could not fetch Sapientia.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. 168.}}}}

'''2.''' A direction from which the wind may blow; a point of the compass, especially one of the cardinal points. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Come from the four ''winds'', O breath, and breathe upon these slain. {{right|Ezek. xxxvii. 9.}}}}

'''3.''' Air artificially put in motion by any force or action: as, the ''wind'' of a bellows; the ''wind'' of a bullet or a cannon-ball (see ''windage'').

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Which he disdaining whisked his sword about,
And with the ''wind'' thereof the king fell down.</poem>
{{right|''Marlowe and Nashe'', Tragedy of Dido, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The whiff and ''wind'' of his fell sword.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, ii. 2. 495.}}}}
'''4.''' Air impregnated with animal odor or scent.
{{smaller block|Else counsellors will but take the ''wind'' of him.
{{right|''Bacon'', Of Counsel.}}}}
'''5.''' In musical instruments the sound of which is produced by a stream of compressed air or breath, either the supply of air under compression, as in the bellows of an organ or in a singer’s lungs, or the stream of air used in sound-production, as in the mouth of an organ-pipe, in the tube of a flageolet, or in the voice.
{{smaller block|<poem>Their instruments were various in their kind,
Some for the bow, and some for breathing ''wind''.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Flower and Leaf, l. 357.}}}}
'''6.''' Breath; also, power of respiration; lung-power. See ''second wind'', below.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ye noye me soore in wastyng al this ''wynde'',
For I haue seide y-noghe, as semethe me.</poem>
{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 79.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>My ''wynde'' is stoppyd, gon is my brethe.</poem>
{{right|''Coventry Mysteries'', p. 226.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Woman, thy wordis and thy ''wynde'' thou not waste.</poem>
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 258.}}}}
{{smaller block|If my ''wind'' were but long enough to say my prayers, I would repent.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., iv. 5. 104.}}}}
{{smaller block|How they spar for ''wind'', instead of hitting from the shoulder.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, ii.}}}}
